    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d319">

                <pb facs="0370" n="270" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg270"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">856d.6363/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Netherlands</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >The Hague</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-07-07T15:00:00+01:19"><hi rendition="#italic">July 7, 1920—3
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 11:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>157. Department’s mail instruction 1162 and my telegram number 150, July
                        2nd<anchor n="7" xml:id="d319-f1"/> regarding oil situation Netherland
                    Indies. For my <pb facs="0371" n="271" xml:id="pg271"/> own guidance in
                    discussing the matter with the Foreign Office I should be glad to know whether
                    the present policy of the Netherlands of “Government reserve” (which however
                    when applied to exploitation agreements makes no apparent distinction between
                    Dutch citizens and foreigners) is a cause for proper retaliation on our part
                    inasmuch as the laws of the United States do not exclude foreigners from
                    participation in the development of the petroleum resources of the United
                    States. Please cable reply.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
